course: space
lesson: 27
title: Telescopes and light
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A telescope is above all a light bucket: its main job is to collect as much light as possible, so faint things show up clearly.
explain: It is like catching rain: a wide bucket catches more drops than a narrow cup, and a wide mirror catches more starlight than your small eye.
explain: Light we see is only one small part of a much wider family of rays, from radio waves to gamma rays, and each kind of telescope "sees" a different part.
explain: Air blurs and blocks some light, so big telescopes sit on high dry mountains, and some telescopes go up into space.
explain: In 1609-1610 Galileo's homemade telescope showed moons going round Jupiter; today giant mirrors and dishes see galaxies near the edge of the visible universe.
fact: In 1609-1610 Galileo used a small homemade telescope to discover four moons of Jupiter, mountains on the Moon and the phases of Venus.
fact: Telescopes gather light either with lenses (refractors) or with curved mirrors (reflectors).
fact: Isaac Newton built an early working reflecting telescope in 1668.
fact: The main job of a telescope is to collect light, so a bigger mirror lets you see fainter and sharper things.
fact: Big telescopes are built on high, dry mountains such as Mauna Kea in Hawaii and the Atacama Desert in Chile.
fact: The Hubble Space Telescope, launched in 1990, orbits above the air, but its mirror was wrongly shaped and astronauts fixed it in 1993.
fact: The James Webb Space Telescope, launched on Christmas Day 2021, has a gold-coated mirror 6.5 metres wide and works about 1.5 million km from Earth, about four times as far away as the Moon.
fact: Light we can see is only a small part of a wider range that includes radio waves, microwaves, infrared, ultraviolet, X-rays and gamma rays.
fact: Radio telescopes are giant dishes that collect radio waves from space, like the Lovell Telescope at Jodrell Bank in Cheshire, 76 metres wide.
fact: The Parkes radio telescope in New South Wales helped relay television pictures of the Apollo 11 Moonwalk to the world in 1969.
fact: X-ray and gamma-ray telescopes have to work in space, because Earth's atmosphere blocks those rays.
fact: The Vera C. Rubin Observatory in Chile released its first images in 2025.
fact: The Extremely Large Telescope, with a 39-metre mirror, is being built in Chile and is planned to see first light around 2030.
fact: Never look at the Sun through a telescope or binoculars: it can blind you in an instant.
exercise: What did Galileo discover with his small telescope? | Four moons of Jupiter | The rings of Neptune | The planet Uranus | The Andromeda Galaxy
exercise: What did Galileo see on our Moon? | Mountains | Oceans of water | Forests | Cities
exercise: What do refracting telescopes use to gather light? | Lenses | Curved mirrors | Radio dishes | Magnets
exercise: Who built an early working reflecting telescope in 1668? | Isaac Newton | Galileo | Edwin Hubble | Stephen Hawking
exercise: What is the main job of a telescope? | To collect light | To make stars bigger and nearer | To heat up the stars | To send signals into space
exercise: Why are big telescopes built on high dry mountains? | There is less air and water vapour to blur the view | They are nearer to the stars up there, so the stars look bigger | Mountain land is far cheaper than land down in the towns | Telescopes only work in freezing cold air, which mountains have
exercise: Name a famous high telescope site in Hawaii. | Mauna Kea | Jodrell Bank | Mount Everest | Parkes
exercise: What went wrong with the Hubble Space Telescope after its 1990 launch? | Its mirror was wrongly shaped | It fell back to Earth | It ran out of fuel at once | It pointed only at the Sun
exercise: When was the James Webb Space Telescope launched? | On Christmas Day 2021 | On New Year's Day 1990 | On 20 July 1969 | It has not launched yet
exercise: How wide is the James Webb mirror? | 6.5 metres | 39 metres | 76 metres | 65 centimetres
exercise: About how far from Earth does the James Webb telescope work? | About 1.5 million km | About 400 km | About 384,000 km | About 150 million km
exercise: Which list contains only kinds of light beyond what we see? | Radio waves, X-rays, gamma rays | Red, green, blue | Sound, heat, wind | Thunder, echoes, music
exercise: What does a radio telescope collect? | Radio waves from space | Music from radio stations | Sound waves from stars | Moon rocks
exercise: How wide is the Lovell Telescope at Jodrell Bank? | 76 metres | 6.5 metres | 7.6 kilometres | 39 metres
exercise: What did the Parkes telescope help do in 1969? | Relay TV pictures of the Apollo 11 Moonwalk | Discover the planet Neptune | Launch Apollo 11 | Find the first exoplanet
exercise: Why must X-ray telescopes work in space? | Earth's atmosphere blocks X-rays | X-rays are too weak on Earth | X-rays only exist near the Moon | X-ray telescopes are too heavy for the ground
exercise: What is being built in Chile for first light around 2030? | The Extremely Large Telescope, with a 39-metre mirror | A new Hubble Space Telescope, to be launched from there | The Lovell radio dish | A telescope on the Moon
test: Noah thinks the main point of a telescope is to make stars look bigger. What is closer to the truth? | Its main job is to collect more light | It mainly brings the stars much nearer to us | It mainly makes the stars hotter so they glow | It mainly changes their colour so we can tell them apart
test: Two telescopes are the same except one mirror is much wider. Which shows fainter stars? | The one with the wider mirror | The one with the narrower mirror | Both exactly the same | Neither shows faint stars
test: hey can i look at the sun with my dad's binoculars quick? | No, never: it can blind you instantly | Yes, if it is only for a second or two | Yes, as long as you wear dark sunglasses | Yes, at sunset it is always safe to look
test: Why did Hubble get blurry pictures at first, and how was it fixed? | Its mirror was wrongly shaped, and astronauts fixed it in 1993 | It was too far away, so it was moved closer | Its lens was dirty, so it was wiped clean by a robot arm sent from Earth | It was never fixed
test: In a cartoon, a scientist in a London back garden takes an X-ray picture of a star with a backyard telescope. What is wrong? | X-rays from space are blocked by the air, so it must be in space | X-rays are only made in hospitals | Stars give out no light at all | Nothing, that works fine as long as the backyard telescope is big enough
test: Which is true about the Lovell Telescope? | It is a radio dish in Cheshire, 76 metres wide | It is a space telescope launched in 2021 to look at the first galaxies | It is a small lens telescope in Hawaii | It is Galileo's homemade telescope
test: Why does the Atacama Desert in Chile host so many telescopes? | It is high and very dry, so the sky is clear | It is the closest place on the Earth to the Moon | It is the only place on Earth where stars can be seen | It never gets dark there, so they can work all day
test: Galileo saw that Venus has phases like the Moon. What does that tell us he used? | A small homemade telescope | A radio dish | A space telescope | The naked eye only
test: James Webb works far from Earth, about 1.5 million km away. Which is closest to that? | About four times the distance to the Moon | About the distance to the Sun | About the height of a plane | About the distance to Mars at its farthest
test: my teacher said the Rubin Observatory is a telescope on the Moon. true? | No, it is in Chile and released its first images in 2025 | Yes, it landed on the Moon in 2025 and took photos from there | Yes, it orbits Mars | No, it is a radio dish in Australia
test: If Earth had no air at all, what could ground telescopes do better? | See sharper and catch X-rays from space | Hear sounds from stars | See through the Sun | Nothing would change at all
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the principal function of an astronomical telescope? | The collection of light from faint objects | The magnification of the heat of stars | The transmission of signals into space | The production of light for observation
exam: Ascertain the reason for which X-ray and gamma-ray telescopes are required to operate in space. | The atmosphere of the Earth blocks such rays | Such rays are produced only near the Moon | Such telescopes are too heavy for the ground | Such rays are too faint at night
exam: Identify the discoveries made by Galileo by means of a telescope in 1609-1610. | Four moons of Jupiter, lunar mountains and the phases of Venus | The planets Uranus and Neptune | The rings of Saturn and the Andromeda Galaxy | The first exoplanet and a black hole
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the James Webb Space Telescope. | It was launched in 2021 and has a gold-coated mirror 6.5 metres wide | It was launched in 1990 and was later repaired by visiting astronauts in 1993 | It is a radio dish of 76 metres in Cheshire | It is under construction in Chile for 2030
